Hash speed on Intel(R) Xeon(R) Gold 6330 CPU @ 2.00GHz (linux7.0.0-38-generic, 112 CPUs), 2026-11-03 09:54:52 UTC
Quick run: 96 rounds; a full run confirms and adds the largest inputs and batches. Each cell is the mean time per unit (total time over total work), lower is better.

In “Messages one after another, buffers owned”, the program hashes one input after another, as fast as it can, alone and with a second program doing the same at once.

SOLO: one copy of each contender, on one thread.

  Messages one after another, buffers owned (ns/B)
  size        B3 servil mt    B3 servil st
  64 KiB           0.0912           0.277 

SHARED, ONE CACHE: the multithreaded contenders, two copies at once on two cores under one last-level cache, each hashing its own input on its own thread, inputs one after another; the time of each copy.

  Messages one after another, buffers owned (ns/B)
  size        B3 servil mt
  64 KiB           0.0935 

SHARED, TWO CACHES: the multithreaded contenders, two copies at once on two cores under two last-level caches, each hashing its own input on its own thread, inputs one after another; the time of each copy.

  Messages one after another, buffers owned (ns/B)
  size        B3 servil mt
  64 KiB            0.107 

SHARED, PLACED BY THE OS: the multithreaded contenders, two copies at once wherever the operating system runs them (it lets no program choose its CPUs here), each hashing its own input on its own thread, inputs one after another; the time of each copy.

